{"name":"Soul Anchor","hex":"#5c1c39","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soul","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The spirit or essence of a person usually thought to consist of one's thoughts and personality, often believed to live on after the person's death.","The spirit or essence of anything."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Characteristic of or pertaining to African American culture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soul"},{"term":"anchor","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tool used to moor a vessel to the bottom of a sea or river to resist movement.","An iron device so shaped as to grip the bottom and hold a vessel at her berth by the chain or rope attached."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To connect an object, especially a ship or a boat, to a fixed point.","To cast anchor; to come to anchor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/anchor"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soul","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and philosophy, the soul is the immaterial aspect or essence of a living being. It is typically believed to be immortal and to exist apart from the material world. Anthropologists and psychologists have found that most humans believe in the existence of a soul or spirit, and that they have cross-culturally distinguished between the soul and the material body."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Schiavonetti_Soul_leaving_body_1808.jpg/330px-Schiavonetti_Soul_leaving_body_1808.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soul"},{"title":"Anchor","blocks":[{"text":"An anchor is a device, normally made of metal, used to secure a vessel to the bed of a body of water to prevent the craft from drifting due to wind or current. The word derives from Latin ancora, which itself comes from the Greek ἄγκυρα."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Kotwica_SS_Pozna%C5%84.JPG/330px-Kotwica_SS_Pozna%C5%84.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchor"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg/500px-The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg/500px-The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bhavachakra describing the cycle of saṃsāra; the wheel of life caused by craving to a self, thus giving raise to rebirths in one of the six realms of existence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg/500px-SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg/500px-SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of a soul being carried to heaven by two angels by William-Adolphe Bouguereau","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg/500px-Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg/1280px-Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg/500px-Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1543 illustration of Muhammad's Night Journey. His ascension to the heavens is often interpreted as an allegory for the human soul ascending to the celestial realms in Islamic philosophy.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMiraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/4DModel.jpg/500px-4DModel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/4DModel.jpg/1280px-4DModel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/4DModel.jpg/500px-4DModel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A visual rendition of the Islamic model of the soul showing the position of nafs relative to other concepts","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A4DModel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Muhammad_ibn_Muhammad_Shakir_Ruzmah-%27i_Nathani_-_A_Soul_Symbolized_as_an_Angel_-_Walters_W65944A_-_Full_Page.jpg/500px-Muhammad_ibn_Muhammad_Shakir_Ruzmah-%27i_Nathani_-_A_Soul_Symbolized_as_an_Angel_-_Walters_W65944A_-_Full_Page.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Muhammad_ibn_Muhammad_Shakir_Ruzmah-%27i_Nathani_-_A_Soul_Symbolized_as_an_Angel_-_Walters_W65944A_-_Full_Page.jpg/500px-Muhammad_ibn_Muhammad_Shakir_Ruzmah-%27i_Nathani_-_A_Soul_Symbolized_as_an_Angel_-_Walters_W65944A_-_Full_Page.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Muhammad ibn Muhammad Shakir Ruzmah-'i Nathani – A Soul Symbolized as an Angel (1717)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuhammad_ibn_Muhammad_Shakir_Ruzmah-'i_Nathani_-_A_Soul_Symbolized_as_an_Angel_-_Walters_W65944A_-_Full_Page.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Atman.jpg/500px-Atman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Atman.jpg/500px-Atman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of the concept of soul (in transmigration) in Jainism. The color gold represents nokarma, the quasi-karmic matter, Cyan depicts dravya karma, the subtle karmic matter, orange represents the bhav karma, the psycho-physical karmic matter, and white depicts sudhatma, the pure consciousness.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtman.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Manunggul_Jar.jpg/500px-Manunggul_Jar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Manunggul_Jar.jpg/1280px-Manunggul_Jar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Manunggul_Jar.jpg/500px-Manunggul_Jar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Neolithic Manunggul burial jar from the Tabon Caves, Palawan, Philippines, depicts a soul and a psychopomp journeying to the spirit world in a boat (c. 890–710 BCE).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AManunggul_Jar.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Socrates%2C_Plato_and_Aristotle.jpg/500px-Socrates%2C_Plato_and_Aristotle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Socrates%2C_Plato_and_Aristotle.jpg/1280px-Socrates%2C_Plato_and_Aristotle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Socrates%2C_Plato_and_Aristotle.jp